ransitions of the corkscrews and the  curves which as bad as they are are  quite realistic so I'll keep them around  and into the last curve and from that  for the brake run braking a little bit  some log brakes at the end and that is  where we could store the second train if  we wanted to have a second trail ice  actually add one to little block  sections and add a second train that's  where that train is going to want to  wait now here's me trying to capture the  classic  arrow coaster filled with a bunch of  scenery around the coaster just very  simple and very rustic but this is the  kind of thing where you usually see  these kinds of coasters either in a  small theme park or somewhere in the  back of a Six Flags park that they don't  really care about em put VR goggles on  um but yeah usually these are pretty  rustic and very nostalgic kind of rides  and also the blog brakes were a bit of  an issue for me at the left table since  the Train actually cleared the circuit  just after the first car reached the  lift here which means that it's going to  have to wait on top of the lift Hill for  the other coaster car to finish the  block that it's on in which case I just  had turned up the waiting time in the  station 280 seconds and that pretty much  fixed that it's not the greatest thing  and it doesn't give you the greatest  stats either the excitement is just  about 4.5 I believe 4.7 but at least the  fear rating and the noise rating are a  bit lower lower than that any general  the ratings are kind of like a family  coaster not too bad it's not too  expensive over ride either and if you  really want to go for a classic Aero  look even in your career or scenario you  could definitely use it now that would  have been all for this video if I wanted  to leave it at just the coaster that I  made but there's one thing that I want  to add and that's that there are also  larger error loopers which came about  especially in the 90s I still standing  example of it is the Viper but most of  them have already been removed which  broke some records for the largest  inversions etc but um they were pretty  weird coasters because they still used  the elements that arrow was using before  that just on a much larger scale so what  you would see with a looping for example  on many of the arrow mega loopers is  that instead of having a completely  large looping by just taking the in-game  loop and drawing it out like this  the loopings would often first have a  large section where it goes up in a very  gentle way going toward the loop for  quite a while and then you would have  the very standard teardrop shaped loop  so to make one right now would be to  just add this large section going up  like that and then when you get to the  actual loop itself start to rotate it a  bit so you can actually go to the side  make the top and make that very small  just get the teardrop shape in there and  the same goes for the other side right  here again it's very small and then  we're going to make the track a bit  longer again and go down to the other  side which is always a bit tricky  actually because you're going to have to  like rotate it to go to the middle right  here and actually go into the other  direction on the other side of the loop  basically it's going to have to rotate  on the top of the looping and you're can  I have to figure out how the looping is  going to go but you generally want a  looping to look like a set when you look  at it from the top so this one doesn't  have enough zette right there  and I'm going to have to add a little  bit more right here that should just  about do it and then a whole lot of  smoothing and correcting some minor  track pieces shouldn't get the teardrop  shape in there at some point and that's  pretty much how these loopings would be  there are some examples of some really  large Aero loopings that are actually  regularly shaped like the Tennessee  tornado but that's getting into some  completely different things I would  actually say if you're interested in  Aero and how they developed and a bit of  general coaster history as well there is  a documentary of the American coaster  enthusiasts called the legacy of arrow  developments which is a really cool  documentary that you can watch if you're  interested and it can certainly teach  you some stuff about roller coasters as  well just a quick tip anyway that's it  for the weird mega looper looping there  are two more elements that I want to  talk about that you could see on arrow  coasters and that you might want to try  and mess with one of them is the batwing  which is very common elements on some of  these mega loopers and it should already  be a standard element in the game which  you can find like this but I have to say  it's a bit of a strange batwing because  many of the real bat wings enter quite a  lot higher than the lowest point of the  batwing so you can also kind of make  your own bat wing in a sense something  like that and then another loop on the  other side back into another  corkscrew as well and that is basically  a batwing there's also the cut back  which is an interesting element that's  found on dragon fire which is a  corkscrew in and then another corkscrew  out in the same direction so you get a  shape like this anyway that's all I  wanted to say about the arrow looping  coaster thank you guys so much for  watching this and if you have any  feedback or comments don't be afraid to  leave them in the comment section of the  video and I'll see you guys in the next  video
 

